<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-switches">
    <title>Digital Inputs: Switches and Debouncing</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to read a pushbutton switch reliably with
            the STM32C031C6, so that one press of a finger produces exactly one
            response from the program.  A pin configured as an input is either HIGH
            or LOW, so reading a button looks as though it should take one line of
            code.  It takes more than that, for two reasons.  The first is that a
            button by itself does not produce a 1 or a 0: it only closes a
            connection from the pin to ground, and a pin with nothing driving it
            reads unpredictably.  The second is that mechanical switches
            <em>bounce</em>: the contacts chatter for a few milliseconds every time
            you press or release them, generating dozens of spurious transitions.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers three class days.  We'll configure a GPIO
            (general-purpose input/output) pin as an input with an internal pull-up
            resistor and read it in a polling loop.  We'll then learn to use the Analog
            Discovery 2 oscilloscope and put it on the button to see the bounce.  We'll
            then get rid of the bounce, in hardware with a capacitor and in software by
            waiting it out.  That still leaves a second problem, which is that polling
            reports the button's level when what a toggle needs is the moment it changes,
            and the pattern that solves it is the <term>state machine</term>.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Configure a GPIO pin as a digital input with an internal pull-up resistor.</li>
                <li>Read the Input Data Register (IDR) and respond to button presses.</li>
                <li>Use the Analog Discovery 2 oscilloscope to observe and measure signals.</li>
                <li>Explain contact bounce and why it is a problem for embedded software.</li>
                <li>Implement hardware debouncing with an RC (resistor-capacitor) filter, and software debouncing that waits out the bounce.</li>
                <li>Use a state machine to act on the moment a button goes down rather than on the fact that it is down.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-switches-concepts">
        <title>Before Class: Switches, Pull-ups, and Bounce</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                In the previous chapter we drove GPIO pins HIGH and LOW from code.
                This reading is about the other direction: a GPIO pin configured as an
                <term>input</term>, where something outside the chip sets the voltage
                on the pin and your code reads it.  A pushbutton is the simplest input
                device there is, and we'll use one for the rest of the chapter, but
                getting a reliable reading out of one takes three things: how to read
                the voltage on a pin at all, how to give the pin a defined voltage
                while the button is not pressed, and what the switch's metal contacts
                do in the few milliseconds while they close.  The three subsections
                below take those in turn.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-reading-a-pin">
            <title>Reading a Pin: The Input Data Register</title>
            <p>
                When a GPIO pin is configured as an input, the STM32C031C6 continuously
                samples the voltage present on that pin and reflects it in the
                <term>Input Data Register</term> (IDR).  Like the ODR (Output Data
                Register), which we used in the previous chapter to drive pins, the
                IDR is a 16-bit register, one bit per pin in the port.  Bit 13 of
                <c>GPIOC-&gt;IDR</c>, for example, reflects the current voltage on PC13,
                which is where the Nucleo board's blue user button is connected.  If the
                pin is at 3.3 V, the bit reads 1; if it is at 0 V, the bit reads 0.
            </p>
            <p>
                The sampling is not done with a plain comparator.  The voltage on the
                pin goes through a <term>Schmitt trigger</term>, a comparator with two
                thresholds instead of one, and its output is what the input data
                register holds.  Configuring the pin as an input is what turns the
                Schmitt trigger on; it also disables the pin's output buffer, and it
                activates whichever of the internal pull-up and pull-down resistors
                <c>GPIOx-&gt;PUPDR</c> selects.  From then on the level on the pin is
                sampled into the input data register on every clock cycle of the
                internal bus, and reading <c>GPIOx-&gt;IDR</c> gives you the most
                recent sample.
            </p>
            <p>
                The Schmitt trigger does not compare the pin voltage against a
                single threshold.  It has two.  Any voltage below
                <term><m>V_{\mathrm{IL}}</m></term> (the maximum low input voltage,
                roughly <m>0.3 \times V_{\mathrm{DD}}</m>, where
                <m>V_{\mathrm{DD}}</m> is the supply voltage, 3.3 V on the Nucleo,
                so about 1.0 V) is reliably read as logic 0, and any voltage above
                <term><m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m></term> (the minimum high input voltage,
                roughly <m>0.7 \times V_{\mathrm{DD}} \approx 2.3\text{ V}</m>) is
                reliably read as logic 1.  In between, the output does not float: the
                Schmitt trigger holds whatever it last settled on, so a rising voltage
                has to reach <m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m> before the pin reads 1 and a
                falling one has to drop to <m>V_{\mathrm{IL}}</m> before it reads 0.
                That gap between the two thresholds is called
                <term>hysteresis</term>, and it is what makes a slow or slightly noisy
                edge produce one clean transition rather than several.
            </p>
            <p>
                In normal digital circuits, where signals are driven cleanly to
                ground or the supply rail, you stay well outside this zone and never
                notice it.  It becomes important when signals are slow to transition
                (for example, a slowly rising voltage from a large RC filter), when
                there is significant noise on a line, or when interfacing devices that
                operate at different supply voltages.
            </p>
            <p>
                Reading a button in code is therefore straightforward, as long as the
                pin voltage is always driven firmly to one rail or the other.  What
                makes a button awkward is not the reading.  It is that while the button
                is open, nothing is driving the pin at all, and that is the subject of
                the next subsection.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-floating-pins">
            <title>Floating Pins and Pull-up Resistors</title>
            <p>
                When a pushbutton is open (not pressed), it disconnects the pin from
                whatever it was connected to.  If nothing else is driving the pin,
                the pin is said to be <term>floating</term>: its voltage is undefined,
                drifting with electrical noise, nearby signals, and even the movement
                of your hand near the board.  Reading a floating pin produces
                unpredictable 0s and 1s that have nothing to do with the button state.
            </p>
            <p>
                The standard fix is a <term>pull-up resistor</term>: a resistor
                connected between the pin and the supply voltage (3.3 V).  When the
                button is open, the resistor pulls the pin to 3.3 V, giving it a
                well-defined logic 1.  When the button is pressed and connects the pin
                to ground, the connection to ground overrides the pull-up, and the pin
                reads logic 0.  This
                <q>active-low</q> convention (pressed = 0, released = 1) is the most
                common arrangement for buttons.
            </p>
            <p>
                Alternatively, a <term>pull-down resistor</term> connects the pin to
                ground, so the unpressed state reads 0 and pressing (which connects to
                3.3 V) reads 1.  The STM32C031C6 has pull-up and pull-down resistors built
                into the chip, selectable per pin via the
                <term>Pull-up/Pull-down Register</term> (PUPDR), so in most cases
                you do not need an external resistor at all.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-contact-bounce">
            <title>Contact Bounce</title>
            <p>
                Even with a stable pull-up, mechanical buttons introduce a second
                problem: <term>contact bounce</term>.  The metal contacts inside a
                switch are tiny springy leaves.  When you press or release the button,
                they do not make clean, instantaneous contact: they collide, rebound,
                collide again, and chatter back and forth for anywhere from a few
                hundred microseconds to a few milliseconds before settling.  During
                this chatter, the pin toggles between 0 and 1 dozens of times in rapid
                succession.
            </p>
            <p>
                To a human finger, a press and release takes tens of milliseconds,
                plenty of time to do something once.  But to a microcontroller running
                at 12 MHz, those milliseconds contain millions of instruction cycles.
                If your code detects a rising or falling edge on the pin, it may see
                50 edges in the time your finger takes to press once.  Depending on
                what action each edge triggers (incrementing a counter, toggling an
                LED, sending a message), this can cause serious errors.
            </p>
            <p>
                Bounce is eliminated either in hardware or in software, and we'll do
                both.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Hardware debouncing</term> adds an RC filter (a resistor and
                capacitor) that smooths out the fast chatter.  The capacitor cannot
                charge or discharge instantaneously, so the rapid bounces are averaged
                away before they reach the pin.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Software debouncing</term> ignores the pin for a short time
                after detecting the first edge, letting the bounce die out before
                sampling again.  A simple approach waits a fixed delay (say, 20 ms)
                after the first transition and then reads the pin's stable final state.
                More robust approaches use a state machine that requires the pin to
                hold a consistent value for several consecutive samples before
                accepting it as a real transition.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-switches-concepts">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-floating-pin">
                <statement><p>A GPIO pin is configured as an input with no pull-up
                or pull-down resistor enabled, and nothing is connected to it.
                What value will <c>GPIOC-&gt;IDR</c> show for that pin?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>An unpredictable value: the pin is floating and
                        its voltage is undefined.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A floating pin picks up electrical noise
                        and nearby signals, so its IDR bit cannot be trusted.  Always
                        enable a pull-up or pull-down, or supply an external one.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Always 0, because an undriven pin defaults to
                        ground.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>An undriven pin does not default to ground: it
                        floats.  Only a pull-down resistor would guarantee a 0.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Always 1, because the STM32C031C6 has an implicit
                        internal pull-up on every pin.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The STM32C031C6's internal pull-ups must be explicitly
                        enabled in PUPDR.  They are off by default.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The last value that was written to the ODR for
                        that pin.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The IDR reflects the voltage present on the pin,
                        not what was last written to the ODR.  In input mode the ODR
                        value is irrelevant to what IDR reads.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-active-low">
                <statement><p>A button connects PC13 to ground when pressed.  An
                internal pull-up is enabled on PC13.  You write
                <c>if (GPIOC-&gt;IDR &amp; (1U &lt;&lt; 13))</c> to detect a press.
                Will this detect the button press correctly?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>No: with an active-low button, pressing gives
                        IDR bit 13 = 0, so the condition is false when pressed and
                        true when released.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The pull-up holds the pin HIGH (1) when
                        open; pressing pulls it LOW (0).  To detect a press, the
                        condition should check for 0, not 1, for example,
                        <c>!(GPIOC-&gt;IDR &amp; (1U &lt;&lt; 13))</c>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Yes: the pull-up drives the pin HIGH when pressed,
                        so bit 13 is 1 and the condition is true.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>It is the opposite: pressing connects the pin to
                        ground, overriding the pull-up and driving the pin LOW (0).
                        The pull-up is active when the button is <em>open</em>,
                        not when it is pressed.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Yes: the IDR always returns 1 for pins with
                        pull-ups enabled, regardless of button state.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The pull-up sets the default state to 1 only when
                        nothing else is driving the pin.  A pressed button actively
                        drives it to 0, overriding the pull-up.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>No: you cannot read a pin that has a pull-up
                        enabled; PUPDR and IDR cannot be used simultaneously.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Pull-ups and IDR work together: that is exactly
                        how buttons are normally wired.  The pull-up sets the default
                        level; IDR reads whatever voltage is currently on the pin.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-bounce-consequence">
                <statement><p>Your code increments a counter each time it
                detects a falling edge on a button pin.  After pressing the button
                once slowly and deliberately, the counter shows 23.  What is the
                most likely explanation?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Contact bounce: the button contacts chattering
                        during the press generated 23 falling edges instead of
                        one.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Without debouncing, each press can
                        produce many edges as the contacts bounce.  The solution is
                        hardware debouncing (RC filter) or software debouncing
                        (ignore edges for ~20 ms after the first one).</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The pull-up resistor value is too large,
                        causing the pin to charge slowly and cross the threshold
                        multiple times.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A large pull-up slows the rise time but does not
                        by itself cause multiple threshold crossings: the signal rises
                        monotonically.  Multiple edges are the signature of
                        bounce.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The microcontroller's clock is too fast, so it
                        samples the IDR more times than expected during a single
                        press.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The clock speed is irrelevant here: the issue is
                        not how often IDR is sampled but how many actual edges appear
                        on the pin due to bouncing contacts.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The counter variable overflowed and wrapped
                        around to 23.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A counter starting at 0 would need to overflow
                        past its maximum value to wrap to 23, unlikely for a single
                        press.  The extra counts come from bounce, not overflow.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>

        <!--
        CANVAS QUIZ: "Before class — Switches, Pull-ups, and Bounce"
        (Due 1 hour before class; auto-graded multiple choice; low stakes.)

        Q1. A GPIO pin configured as an input has no pull-up, no pull-down, and
            nothing connected to it. What does the corresponding IDR bit reliably tell you?
            A) The pin is HIGH (logic 1).
            B) The pin is LOW (logic 0).
            C) Nothing — the pin is floating and the bit value is unpredictable.  ← CORRECT
            D) The same value that was last written to the ODR.

        Q2. A button connects PA0 to ground when pressed. An internal pull-up is
            enabled on PA0. What is the IDR bit value for PA0 when the button is pressed?
            A) 1, because the pull-up holds it HIGH.
            B) 0, because the button connects the pin to ground, overriding the pull-up.  ← CORRECT
            C) Floating — you cannot predict it.
            D) Alternating 0 and 1 rapidly.

        Q3. You need a GPIO pin to read 0 when idle and 1 when a button is pressed
            (active-high convention). Which internal resistor mode should you enable?
            A) Pull-up (PUPDR = 01).
            B) Pull-down (PUPDR = 10).  ← CORRECT
            C) No pull (PUPDR = 00).
            D) Analog mode.

        Q4. Contact bounce typically lasts:
            A) Several seconds.
            B) Microseconds to a few milliseconds.  ← CORRECT
            C) Exactly one clock cycle of the microcontroller.
            D) As long as the button is held down.

        Q5. A hardware RC debounce filter uses a resistor and capacitor in series
            between the button and the GPIO pin. What does the capacitor do?
            A) It amplifies the signal so the MCU can read it more easily.
            B) It stores the last button state so the MCU doesn't need to poll.
            C) It slows down voltage changes, smoothing out the rapid bounces so
               the pin sees a single clean transition.  ← CORRECT
            D) It provides the pull-up current so no internal pull-up is needed.
        -->

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         DAY 3 IN-CLASS  (Tuesday, 110 min)
         The part budgets below sum to 70 minutes, which leaves slack in a
         110-minute Tuesday; how the rest of the hour is spent is the
         instructor's call on the day.  Do not "correct" the class length to
         match the sum: CLAUDE.md fixes Day N with N odd at 110 minutes.
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-switches-day3">
        <title>Day 3 In-Class: Digital Inputs and the Oscilloscope</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll start reading the outside world with the STM32C031C6.
                We'll open with a code review of the counter you wrote for Lab 1.
                Then we'll set up the Analog Discovery 2 oscilloscope on the LEDs that
                program is already blinking, so that we have an instrument for seeing
                what a pin is actually doing.  After that comes the button itself:
                we'll wire it to one of the Nucleo's pins, find in the reference manual
                (<url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url>) the bits that make
                a pin an input, and read it in code.  The last part of the hour is a
                set of button exercises that are the start of your Lab 2 work.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- ── Part 1: Code Review (≈10 min) ──────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day3-code-review">
            <title>Part 1: Code Review (blinkyCNT.c)</title>
            <p>
                We'll start with your <c>blinkyCNT.c</c> from Lab 1.  Share it within
                your table group, read each other's solutions, and explain the approach
                you used to display a 3-bit binary count on three LEDs.  Reading
                somebody else's code and being able to explain it back is what a code
                review is.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day3-code-review">
                <title>blinkyCNT Code Review</title>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Read your partners' code.  Can you follow the logic?
                        For each solution you read, identify how bits are placed
                        into the correct LED positions in <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c>.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Compare the approach in the code below with your own.
                        What does the shift <c>count &lt;&lt; LED1_BIT</c> accomplish?
                        Why is it necessary to clear the LED bits first?
                    </p>
                    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Clear the three LED bits, then write the new count
GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_ALL;
GPIOA->ODR |= (count << LED1_BIT);
]]></code></program>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <!-- Self-contained slide, NOT a ref to act-day3-code-review: the
                 player strips <program> out of an activity it projects, so a
                 plain activity ref would drop the very code the questions ask
                 students to compare against. (AUTHORING-slides.md; the Day 6
                 macro rewrite is the same pattern.) -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-code-review">
                <p>Read each other's <c>blinkyCNT.c</c> at your table, then compare with the approach below:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Clear the three LED bits, then write the new count
GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_ALL;
GPIOA->ODR |= (count << LED1_BIT);
]]></code></program>
                <ol>
                    <li>In each solution you read, how do the count's bits end up in the correct LED positions in <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c>?</li>
                    <li>What does the shift <c>count &lt;&lt; LED1_BIT</c> accomplish?</li>
                    <li>Why is it necessary to clear the LED bits first?</li>
                </ol>
            </slide>

            <!-- Instructor solution listings for the Lab 1 blinky programs.
                 Inlined in <slide> blocks so they are hidden from the reading
                 book but projected in class. (Programs are not ref-targetable in
                 PreTeXt, so code lives inline; figures/activities use ref.) -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-sim-solution" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN   (1U<<0)     // bit 0 enables clock for GPIOA
// LEDs on D13, D12, D11 (PA5, 6, 7)
#define LED_PIN1  (1U<<5)
#define LED_PIN2  (1U<<6)
#define LED_PIN3  (1U<<7)
#define LED_ALL   (LED_PIN1 | LED_PIN2 | LED_PIN3)
int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOAEN;                       // 1. clock access to GPIOA
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10)|(1U<<12)|(1U<<14);   // 2. PA5,6,7 as outputs
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11) & ~(1U<<13) & ~(1U<<15);
    while (1) {
        GPIOA->ODR |=  LED_ALL;   // 3. turn LEDs on
        delay_ms(500);
        GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_ALL;   // 4. turn LEDs off
        delay_ms(500);
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-seq-solution" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN   (1U<<0)
#define LED_PIN1  (1U<<5)
#define LED_PIN2  (1U<<6)
#define LED_PIN3  (1U<<7)
int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOAEN;                       // clock access to GPIOA
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10)|(1U<<12)|(1U<<14);   // PA5,6,7 as outputs
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11) & ~(1U<<13) & ~(1U<<15);
    while (1) {
        GPIOA->ODR = (GPIOA->ODR & ~LED_PIN3) | LED_PIN1;  // -> LED1
        delay_ms(500);
        GPIOA->ODR = (GPIOA->ODR & ~LED_PIN1) | LED_PIN2;  // -> LED2
        delay_ms(500);
        GPIOA->ODR = (GPIOA->ODR & ~LED_PIN2) | LED_PIN3;  // -> LED3
        delay_ms(500);
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-cnt-solution" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN   (1U<<0)
#define LED1_BIT  5
#define LED_PIN1  (1U<<LED1_BIT)
#define LED_PIN2  (1U<<(LED1_BIT+1))
#define LED_PIN3  (1U<<(LED1_BIT+2))
#define LED_ALL   (LED_PIN1 | LED_PIN2 | LED_PIN3)
int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOAEN;                       // clock access to GPIOA
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10)|(1U<<12)|(1U<<14);   // PA5,6,7 as outputs
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11) & ~(1U<<13) & ~(1U<<15);
    while (1) {
        for (unsigned count = 0; count < 8; count++) {
            GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_ALL;          // turn all LEDs off
            GPIOA->ODR |= count << LED1_BIT; // write the count
            delay_ms(500);
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 2: AD2 Oscilloscope (≈20 min) ─────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day3-ad2">
            <title>Part 2: The Analog Discovery 2 Oscilloscope</title>
            <p>
                Next we'll watch a signal change in time.  The instrument we'll use
                all term for this is the <term>Analog Discovery 2</term> (AD2), a
                compact USB instrument that combines a two-channel oscilloscope, a
                waveform generator, a logic analyzer, and more in a pocket-sized
                package.  Plug it into your laptop and open the <em>Waveforms</em>
                application: it shows you the actual voltages on your circuit as
                they change.  We'll point it at the LEDs of a blinky program first,
                where we already know how wide a pulse should be.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ad2-photo">
                <caption>The Analog Discovery 2 (AD2).  The USB port connects
                to a laptop running Waveforms.  The multicolored
                <q>flying lead</q> cables connect to your breadboard and Nucleo
                board.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide16_31ca4cb7.png"
                       width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-ad2-photo" ref="fig-ad2-photo">
                <ul>
                    <li>A compact USB instrument: oscilloscope, waveform generator, logic analyzer, and more.</li>
                    <li>Plug it into your laptop and open the <em>Waveforms</em> application.</li>
                    <li>It shows you the actual voltages on your circuit as they change.</li>
                </ul>
                            <caption>The AD2 connects to a laptop over USB; flying-lead cables reach your breadboard.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                The AD2 connects to your circuit through flexible
                <term>flying leads</term>, color-coded wires that plug directly
                into breadboard holes.  The two oscilloscope channels are labeled
                <c>1+</c>/<c>1−</c> (orange) and <c>2+</c>/<c>2−</c> (blue).
                The minus leads have a white stripe.  Always connect these to your
                circuit's GND rail.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ad2-pinout">
                <caption>AD2 flying-lead connector pinout.  Channels 1 and 2
                each have a plus (signal) lead and a minus (ground reference)
                lead with a white stripe.  Additional leads support the waveform
                generator, digital I/O, and power supply, covered in later
                labs.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide17_e3a60d59.png"
                       width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <!-- Condensed in-class slide for the AD2 pinout. Shows fig-ad2-pinout
                 (image not duplicated) with these talking points; the full prose
                 above stays in the reading view only. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-ad2-pinout" ref="fig-ad2-pinout">
                <ul>
                    <li>Color-coded flying leads plug straight into breadboard holes.</li>
                    <li><clr c="orange">Channel 1 = 1+ / 1−  (orange)</clr></li>
                    <li><clr c="blue">Channel 2 = 2+ / 2−  (blue)</clr></li>
                    <li>The minus leads have a white stripe.  Always connect these to GND.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>The − leads are the ground reference, not a second signal — students always try to wire them to a second pin.</note>
                            <caption>Each channel has a plus (signal) and a minus (ground-reference) lead.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                We'll connect both minus leads to GND, then channel 1's plus lead to
                one LED anode and channel 2's plus lead to a second
                (<xref ref="fig-ad2-wiring"/>), with <c>blinkySEQ.c</c> running on the
                Nucleo so that there is something to look at.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ad2-wiring">
                <caption>Wiring the AD2 to observe two of the three LEDs from
                Lab 1.  The minus (white-stripe) leads connect to ground; the
                plus leads of channels 1 and 2 connect to two LED
                anodes.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide18_b0119462.png"
                       width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-ad2-wiring" ref="fig-ad2-wiring">
                <ul>
                    <li>Load <c>blinkySEQ.c</c> onto your Nucleo.</li>
                    <li>Connect both minus (white-stripe) leads to GND.</li>
                    <li>CH1 (+) → one LED anode; CH2 (+) → a second LED anode.</li>
                </ul>
                            <caption>Minus leads to ground; CH1 and CH2 plus leads to two LED anodes.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                Open <em>Scope</em> in Waveforms and press <em>Run</em>.  Four controls
                do most of the work.  <term>Time Base</term> sets how much time fits
                across the screen: 100 ms/div shows a full blink cycle, and 20 µs/div
                is where switch bounce becomes visible.  <term>Range</term> sets the
                vertical scale in volts per division; adjust it until the waveform
                fills roughly half the screen height.  <term>Offset</term> shifts one
                channel up or down, which is how you separate two traces that would
                otherwise overlap.  <term>Trigger</term> starts the capture when a
                signal crosses a threshold, and that is what lets you catch a single
                button press.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ad2-waveform">
                <caption>Waveforms scope view of a sequentially-blinking LED
                program.  Orange (CH1) and blue (CH2) pulses alternate; each is
                about 100 ms wide.  The settings panel (right) shows Time Base
                at 100 ms/div, both channels at 2 V/div, with an offset on CH2
                to keep the traces from overlapping.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="68% 28%" margins="2%">
                    <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide19_5c4e2225.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide19_settings_panel.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-ad2-debrief" ref="fig-ad2-waveform">
                <ul>
                    <li><term>Time Base</term>: time across the screen (100 ms/div for a blink; 20 µs/div for bounce).</li>
                    <li><term>Range</term>: vertical scale, V/div; fill about half the height.</li>
                    <li><term>Offset</term>: shift a channel to separate overlapping traces.</li>
                    <li><term>Trigger</term>: start capture when a signal crosses a threshold.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>REVEAL after the Exploring activity — confirm what students discovered about each control.</note>
                            <caption>CH1 (orange) &amp; CH2 (blue) pulses, ~100 ms wide; settings panel at right.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                To measure a time interval precisely, use the
                <term>cursor</term> tool: drag two vertical cursor lines to the
                rising and falling edges of a pulse and the toolbar shows the
                time difference between them.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ad2-cursors">
                <caption>Using the Waveforms cursor tool to measure pulse width.
                Two vertical cursors bracket one LED pulse; the toolbar shows
                the time difference.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/slide04_9157d76f.png"
                       width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-ad2-cursors" ref="fig-ad2-cursors">
                <ul>
                    <li>Drag two vertical cursors to the rising and falling edges of a pulse.</li>
                    <li>The toolbar shows the time difference (Δt) between them.</li>
                    <li>We'll use this to measure pulse widths today, and tomorrow we'll use it to measure how long a switch bounces for.</li>
                </ul>
                            <caption>Two cursors bracket one LED pulse; the toolbar reads the time difference.</caption>
            </slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day3-ad2-explore">
                <title>Exploring the Oscilloscope</title>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Run the scope and observe both channels.  Then experiment
                        with the following settings and answer each question at
                        your table:
                    </p>
                    <p><ol>
                        <li><p>What does <em>Time Base</em> control?  What value shows one full blink cycle across the screen?</p></li>
                        <li><p>What does <em>Range</em> control?  Set it so the waveform fills roughly half the vertical space.</p></li>
                        <li><p>What does <em>Offset</em> do?  How do you use it to separate two overlapping channels?</p></li>
                        <li><p>What voltage does the scope measure on a GPIO pin when the LED connected to it is on?</p></li>
                    </ol></p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Measure the pulse width of one LED's ON period using
                        the cursor tool.  Place two vertical cursors at the
                        rising and falling edges of a single pulse and read the
                        time difference from the toolbar.  Does it match the
                        <c>delay_ms</c> value in your code?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Open your <c>blinkySIM</c> project, change the delays
                        to 1 ms ON / 10 ms OFF, and flash it to your Nucleo.
                        Observe the LED brightness and the waveform on the scope.
                        Then swap to 10 ms ON / 1 ms OFF and flash again.  The
                        oscilloscope can see the LED going on and off even when
                        your eyes cannot.  Discuss at your table why the
                        brightness changes, and where that could be useful.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        You want to observe a switch bounce that lasts about
                        1 ms.  Which time base setting would you choose, and
                        why?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Save a screenshot of your scope view:
                        <em>File → Export → Image tab → Save</em>.
                        You will need this skill for all future lab reports.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 3: Digital Inputs (≈25 min) ───────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day3-inputs">
            <title>Part 3: Wiring and Reading a Digital Input</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll use a pin for input rather than output.  We'll add a
                pushbutton to the breadboard (<xref ref="fig-pushbutton-wiring"/>).
                Use <em>D5 (PB4)</em> specifically, since the code we'll start with,
                <c>button.c</c>, is written for PB4.  Wire one side of the button to D5 and the
                other side to GND.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-pushbutton-wiring">
                <caption>Breadboard wiring for a pushbutton connected to a
                GPIO input.  The button bridges two rows of the breadboard;
                one terminal goes to the GPIO pin, the other to GND.  The
                Nucleo's internal pull-up resistor keeps the pin HIGH when
                the button is open.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide23_cdfa0eb4.png"
                       width="65%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-pushbutton-wiring" ref="fig-pushbutton-wiring">
                <ul>
                    <li>Add a pushbutton to your Lab 1 breadboard.</li>
                    <li>Wire one side to D5, the other side to GND.</li>
                    <li>Use <em>D5 (PB4)</em> specifically, since the code we'll start with, <c>button.c</c>, is written for PB4.</li>
                </ul>
                            <caption>The button bridges two rows: one terminal to the GPIO pin, the other to GND.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                A button by itself does not produce a 1 or a 0: it only closes a
                connection from the pin to ground.  With nothing else connected the
                pin <term>floats</term>, its voltage drifts with electrical noise, and
                reading it produces random 0s and 1s.  The fix is to pull the pin up,
                so that it reads HIGH while the switch is unpressed.  A
                <term>pull-up resistor</term> between the pin and 3.3 V does that:
                while the button is open the resistor holds the pin at 3.3 V and
                <c>GPIOB-&gt;IDR</c> reads 1, and pressing the button connects the pin
                to ground, which overrides the pull-up so the pin reads 0.  That is the
                <term>active-low</term> convention, pressed = 0 and released = 1, and
                it is the standard arrangement for buttons.  There are two ways to
                supply the resistor: an external one of about 10 kΩ, or the
                STM32C031C6's own internal pull-up of about 40 kΩ, which we enable in
                software.  We'll use the internal one, so nothing extra goes on the
                breadboard.  If something else is actively driving the pin, the pull-up
                has no effect.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-pushbutton-schematic">
                <caption>Alternative wiring using an <em>external</em>
                pull-up resistor between the power rail and the button.
                The resistor holds the pin HIGH when the button is open;
                pressing the button pulls it LOW.  On the STM32C031C6 we use
                the built-in internal pull-up instead, so no external
                resistor is needed.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide24_07d739fd.png"
                       width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-pushbutton-active-low" ref="fig-pushbutton-schematic">
                <ul>
                    <li>A button by itself does not produce a 1 or a 0: it only closes a connection from the pin to ground. With nothing else connected the pin <term>floats</term>, drifting with noise and reading random 0s and 1s. So we pull the pin up with a resistor to 3.3 V, and it reads HIGH while the switch is unpressed.</li>
                    <li>Pressing the button connects the pin to ground, which overrides the pull-up, so the pin reads LOW. That is the <term>active-low</term> convention: pressed = 0, released = 1. If something else is actively driving the pin, the pull-up has no effect.</li>
                    <li>Two ways to supply the resistor: an external one of about 10 kΩ, or the STM32C031C6's own internal pull-up of about 40 kΩ, which we enable in software. We'll use the internal one.</li>
                </ul>
                            <caption>An external pull-up shown for intuition; on the STM32C031C6 we use the internal one.</caption>
            </slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-io-port-bit">
                <caption>One GPIO bit: read path (pin → Schmitt trigger → IDR) and write path (ODR → output control → pin).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/ann-22.png"
                       width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <!-- Diagram-dominant slides: no bullets, so the deck renders the
                 figure large for the instructor to talk over. These three views
                 of the same port-bit diagram are each shown full-size. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-portbit" ref="fig-io-port-bit">                <caption>Read path (input): pin → pull-up/down → Schmitt trigger → IDR.  Write path (output): ODR → output control → pin.  The same pull-up/pull-down serve both modes.  The Schmitt trigger is a comparator with two thresholds rather than one, and its output is what IDR holds.</caption>
            </slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-switch-port-bit">
                <caption>The same bit wired to a button: the internal pull-up holds the pin HIGH when the switch is open; closing it pulls the pin LOW (blue path into IDR).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/ann-24.png"
                       width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-switch-portbit" ref="fig-switch-port-bit">                <caption>The same port bit, now wired to a button: the internal pull-up holds the pin HIGH when the switch is open; closing the switch pulls the pin to GND (LOW). The blue path shows the level flowing into IDR.</caption>
            </slide>

            <p>
                Three registers control GPIO input behavior on the STM32C031C6.
                <c>GPIOx-&gt;MODER</c>, the Mode Register for GPIO port x (where x is
                the port letter: A, B, C, D or F), sets the pin mode: input, output,
                alternate function or analog.  <c>GPIOx-&gt;PUPDR</c> turns the
                internal pull-up or pull-down on or off.  <c>GPIOx-&gt;IDR</c> is
                read-only, and each of its bits reflects the current logic level on the
                corresponding pin.  The specific bit values for each setting are in the
                reference manual
                (<url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url>), and we'll look
                them up there rather than take them from a slide.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-input-config-diagram">
                <caption>Registers for a GPIO input: MODER (mode), PUPDR (pull-up/down), IDR (live level). Blue path: button → pull-up → Schmitt trigger → IDR.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/ann-23.png"
                       width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-input-config" ref="fig-input-config-diagram">                <caption>The blue path: button → pull-up → Schmitt trigger → IDR.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-pb4-config">
                <p>Here is how to configure PB4 as an input:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// PB4 = D5, active-low button with internal pull-up
#define GPIOBEN    (1U << 1)   // IOPENR bit 1 -> GPIOB clock
#define BUTTON_PIN (1U << 4)   // PB4 = D5
RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOBEN;        // 1. enable GPIOB clock
GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U << 8) & ~(1U << 9); // 2. MODER [9:8] = 00 -> input
                               //    (pins reset to 11, analog — clear both bits)
GPIOB->PUPDR |=  (1U << 8);    // 3. PUPDR [9:8] = 01 -> pull-up
GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U << 9);
]]></code></program>
                <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL — show after the reference-manual activity if useful, or skip if the class is on track.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-vil-vih">
                <p>The Schmitt trigger does not compare the pin voltage against a single threshold.  It has two:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Any voltage below <m>V_{\mathrm{IL}}</m>, the maximum low input voltage, is reliably read as a logic 0.  That is about <m>0.3 \times V_{\mathrm{DD}}</m>, where <m>V_{\mathrm{DD}}</m> is the supply voltage, 3.3 V on the Nucleo, so about 1.0 V.</li>
                    <li>Any voltage above <m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m>, the minimum high input voltage (about <m>0.7 \times V_{\mathrm{DD}} \approx 2.3</m> V), is reliably read as a logic 1.</li>
                    <li>Between the two, the Schmitt trigger holds whatever it last settled on: a rising voltage has to reach <m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m> before it is read as 1, and a falling one has to drop to <m>V_{\mathrm{IL}}</m> before it is read as 0.</li>
                    <li>That gap between the two thresholds is <term>hysteresis</term>, and it is why a slow or slightly noisy edge still produces one clean transition instead of several.</li>
                    <li>In ordinary digital circuits the signal is driven firmly to one rail or the other, so you stay well outside that zone.  It matters when a signal rises slowly, for example through a large RC filter, or when there is significant noise on the line.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL.</note>
            </slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day3-ref-manual">
                <title>Finding Configuration Details in the Reference Manual</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        You know what MODER, PUPDR, and IDR do, but not yet
                        the specific bit values to write.  Open the
                        reference manual (RM0490),
                        navigate to the <em>General-purpose I/Os (GPIO)</em>
                        section via the table of contents, then go to
                        <em>RM0490 §6.4, GPIO registers</em>.
                        Looking up register details in the reference manual is
                        something you will keep doing in this course.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        One note on naming before you start.
                        <c>GPIOx_MODER</c> is the register's name in the
                        reference manual.  <c>GPIOx-&gt;MODER</c> is a
                        dereferenced pointer to the memory location that
                        register is mapped to, so to change
                        <c>GPIOx_MODER</c> we write to
                        <c>GPIOx-&gt;MODER</c>, and that is the form we'll
                        always use in code.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Find <c>GPIOx_MODER</c> (RM0490 §6.4.1).  What
                        two-bit value selects input mode?  What is the
                        power-on default for all pins?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Find <c>GPIOx_PUPDR</c>.  Which two-bit value enables
                        the internal pull-up?  Write the C statements that
                        enable the pull-up on PB4 (pin 4 of Port B), using
                        bit masks.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-button-excerpt">
                <p>This is the part of <c>button.c</c> that reads the pin.  It enables the pull-up, waits for the pin to settle, and then lights the LED for as long as the button is held down:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// PB4 = D5 button, PA5 = D13 LED (active-low button)
GPIOB->PUPDR |=  (1U << 8);   // enable pull-up on PB4
GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U << 9);
delay_ms(50);                 // give the pull-up time to pull the pin HIGH
while (1) {
    if ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0)  // active-low: 0 = pressed
        GPIOA->ODR |=  LED_PIN;          // LED on
    else
        GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;          // LED off
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

            <activity xml:id="act-day3-button-c">
                <title>Reading a Button in Code</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        In STM32CubeIDE, make a copy of your
                        <c>TemplateProject</c> and name the copy
                        <c>Buttons</c>.
                        Then download <c>button.c</c> from Canvas and place it
                        in the <c>Src</c> folder of your new project.
                        Read through the code, then answer the questions below
                        before flashing it.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        The code calls <c>delay_ms(50)</c> immediately after
                        enabling the pull-up resistor.  Why?  What do you
                        think would happen if you removed that line?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        The main loop uses
                        <c>(GPIOB-&gt;IDR &amp; BUTTON_PIN) == 0</c>
                        to detect a button press.  Why <c>== 0</c> rather
                        than <c>!= 0</c>?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Flash the code and verify: pressing the button turns
                        on the LED; releasing turns it off.  Then modify
                        <c>blinkySEQ.c</c> so that pressing the button
                        interrupts the sequence and lights all three LEDs;
                        releasing returns to the sequence.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-pullup-settling">
                <caption>Oscilloscope trace of the pin voltage rising after
                the internal pull-up is enabled.  The pin reaches the HIGH
                threshold in about 2.5 µs; reading IDR before it settles
                returns a spurious 0.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03-Switches/slide36_428a4d33.png"
                       width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-pullup-settling" ref="fig-pullup-settling">
                <ul>
                    <li>The pin does not jump to HIGH instantly: the pull-up charges the pin capacitance.</li>
                    <li>It reaches the HIGH threshold in about 2.5 µs.</li>
                    <li>If you read IDR before it settles you get a spurious 0, so delay first.</li>
                </ul>
                            <caption>Pin voltage rising after the internal pull-up is enabled: ~2.5 µs to reach HIGH.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-debounce-preview">
                <p>Tomorrow we'll be adding a capacitor across the button to suppress the bounce.  That makes the button behave better, and it makes this settling problem worse:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>With a 0.1 µF capacitor the pin takes ~4 ms to reach HIGH.</li>
                    <li>That leaves even more time to accidentally read the old 0.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL — preview of Day 3x.</note>
            </slide>

            <p>
                The general problem is that code executes far faster than a pin can
                change state.  The pins on the chip start out at 0, and enabling the
                pull-up does not make the pin read 1 until the pin's stray capacitance
                has charged; the debounce capacitor we'll add tomorrow makes that take
                longer still.  There are several ways to deal with it.  The easiest is
                the one <c>button.c</c> uses: wait 50 ms with <c>delay_ms()</c> after
                initialization, before sampling any pin.  You can also loop until the
                pin reads 1; or start the pin as an output, drive it to 1, and then
                switch it to input and enable the pull-up; or fit an external pull-up
                instead of using the internal one, which helps only because an external
                one can be far smaller than the internal 40 kΩ and so recharges the pin
                faster.
            </p>
            <p>
                None of those removes the problem, because it is not only a start-up
                problem.  It happens any time the pin is pulled down, so your code has
                to account for it every time it reads a button.  A smaller external
                pull-up only shortens the wait, and once the debounce capacitor is
                fitted it works against the debouncing.  The reliable way to do that in ordinary code is a state
                machine that acts only on a transition: make sure the program has gone
                from PRESSED back to UNPRESSED before it takes another action.  That is
                the pattern we'll build on Thursday.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3-settling-fixes">
                <p>The pins start out at 0, and enabling the pull-up does not make a pin read 1 until its stray capacitance has charged.  Waiting, as <c>button.c</c> does, is the easiest way to deal with it.  There are others:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Loop until the pin reads 1.</li>
                    <li>Start the pin as an output, drive it to 1, then switch it to input and enable the pull-up.</li>
                    <li>Fit an external pull-up instead of using the internal one.  This helps only because an external one can be far smaller than the internal 40 kΩ, so it recharges the pin faster.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>None of those three removes the problem, because it is not only a start-up problem: it happens any time the pin is pulled down, so your code has to account for it on every read.  A smaller external pull-up only shortens the wait, and once the debounce capacitor is fitted tomorrow it works against the debouncing.  The reliable way in ordinary code is a state machine that acts only on a transition, which is the pattern we'll build on Thursday.</p>
                <note>The bridge from today's settling problem to Thursday's state machine, and worth the two minutes even when the hour is tight.</note>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 4: Button Exercises (≈15 min) ─────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day3-button-exercises">
            <title>Part 4: Button Exercises</title>
            <p>
                In the rest of the hour we'll put the button to work on the programs
                from Lab 1.  These exercises are part of your Lab 2 work.  You will not
                finish them in class, but they are a good head start.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day3-button-exercises">
                <title>Button Exercises</title>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Modify <c>blinkyCNT.c</c> so that pressing the button
                        pauses the counter and releasing resumes it.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        <em>Homework (due Thursday):</em>
                        Write <c>toggleLED.c</c> so the on-board LED toggles
                        each time the button is pressed and released:
                        first press turns LED on, second press turns it off,
                        and so on.  You will likely observe erratic behavior
                        (<q>glitches</q>).  Bring your observations to class
                        tomorrow.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                The simulator below is seeded with <c>button.c</c> as you ran it
                in class, so you can start the toggle homework tonight whether
                or not your Nucleo is in front of you.  Attach the button first:
                click <em>+ Button</em> in the component bay, then click the Arduino
                header pin D5.  <em>Step</em> walks the program one line at a time, and
                the register panel shows <c>PUPDR</c> and <c>IDR</c>, so you can
                watch the pin read 1 released and 0 pressed.  One warning: the
                simulated button makes clean contact, so the glitches you are
                asked to observe are only partly reproduced here.  You will see
                the LED flip on every pass of the loop while the button is held,
                which is the larger of the two effects, but contact bounce needs
                the real switch.
            </p>

            <sim starter="toggleLEDstart"/>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day3-pause">
                <p>
                    The pause/resume exercise, on top of <c>blinkyCNT.c</c>.  The
                    counter is on PA5–PA7 already; what is new is that the button
                    is on port B, so GPIOB needs its own clock, its input mode
                    and its pull-up.  Pausing itself is one line: while the pin
                    reads LOW, stay put.
                </p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN    (1U<<0)
#define GPIOBEN    (1U<<1)          // the button is on port B, so its clock too
#define LED1_BIT   5
#define LED_PIN1   (1U<<LED1_BIT)
#define LED_PIN2   (1U<<(LED1_BIT+1))
#define LED_PIN3   (1U<<(LED1_BIT+2))
#define LED_ALL    (LED_PIN1 | LED_PIN2 | LED_PIN3)
#define BUTTON_PIN (1U<<4)          // PB4 / D5

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR |= (GPIOAEN | GPIOBEN);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10)|(1U<<12)|(1U<<14);        // PA5,6,7 outputs
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11) & ~(1U<<13) & ~(1U<<15);
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U<<8) & ~(1U<<9);               // PB4 input
    GPIOB->PUPDR |= (1U<<8);                           // PB4 pull-up
    GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U<<9);
    while (1) {
        for (unsigned count = 0; count < 8; count++) {
            GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_ALL;
            GPIOA->ODR |= count << LED1_BIT;
            delay_ms(500);
            while ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);    // held: wait here
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
                <p>
                    The pause is <em>level</em>-triggered: it holds while the
                    button is down and resumes when it comes up, so no edge
                    detection is needed and no bounce is visible, which is why
                    this exercise comes before <c>toggleLED.c</c> rather than
                    after it.  The wait is also a blocking loop: the program can
                    do nothing else while a finger is on the button.  That is
                    exactly the structure we'll replace with a state machine on
                    Thursday, and with an interrupt on Day 9.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The homework, <c>toggleLED.c</c>, has its solutions already:
                    the edge-detected version on Day 3x and both of the structures
                    students bring in on Day 4.
                </p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

    </section>



    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-gpio-input-config">
        <title>Configuring a Pin as an Input</title>
        <p>
            Every GPIO pin on the STM32C031C6 can be configured as a digital input, digital
            output, analog input, or alternate function.  For a pushbutton we use digital
            input mode.  The internal structure of one I/O port bit is shown in
            <xref ref="fig-io-port-bit"/>: an optional internal pull-up or pull-down
            resistor, a read path (IDR), and a write path (ODR).
        </p>

        <p>
            Three register fields configure a pin as an input.
        </p>
        <p>
            <c>GPIOx-&gt;MODER</c>, the Mode Register, has two bits per pin, and writing
            <c>00</c> into them selects input mode.  Do not assume those bits are
            already <c>00</c>: pins reset to <c>11</c>, analog mode (the reference
            manual, <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url> §6.4.1, reset
            value 0xEBFF FFFF for port A and 0xFFFF FFFF for the other ports), so clear
            both bits explicitly for every input pin.
        </p>
        <p>
            <c>GPIOx-&gt;PUPDR</c> also has two bits per pin.  Writing <c>01</c> enables
            the internal pull-up, <c>10</c> enables the pull-down, and <c>00</c> leaves
            the pin floating.
        </p>
        <p>
            <c>GPIOx-&gt;IDR</c> is read-only, and bit <m>n</m> of it reflects the
            current logic level on pin <m>n</m>.
        </p>

        <insight>
            <title>Why use a pull-up resistor?</title>
            <p>
                A floating input pin picks up electrical noise and reads unpredictably.
                Connecting a <term>pull-up resistor</term>, from the pin to the 3.3 V
                supply, makes the idle state HIGH.  When the button closes, it connects the pin to GND, pulling
                it LOW.  This is called <term>active-low</term> logic: pressed = 0, released = 1.
                The STM32C031C6's built-in pull-up resistors are typically 40 kΩ,
                so no external resistor is needed.
                <xref ref="fig-pushbutton-wiring"/> shows the breadboard
                wiring using the internal pull-up.
                <xref ref="fig-pushbutton-schematic"/> shows the alternative:
                an external pull-up resistor on the breadboard.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <p>
            Setting up PB4 (D5 on the Nucleo) as an input with pull-up enabled,
            as in <c>button.c</c>:
        </p>
        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define GPIOBEN    (1U << 1)   // bit 1 of IOPENR enables GPIOB clock
#define BUTTON_PIN (1U << 4)   // PB4 = D5

// 1. Enable GPIOB clock
RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOBEN;

// 2. Configure PB4 as input: MODER bits [9:8] = 00
//    Pins reset to analog (11), so clear both bits explicitly
GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U << 8) & ~(1U << 9);

// 3. Enable internal pull-up on PB4
//    PUPDR bits [9:8]: 01 = pull-up
GPIOB->PUPDR |=  (1U << 8);   // set bit 8
GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U << 9);   // clear bit 9
]]></code></program>

        <p>
            After a short settling time the pin reads HIGH when the button is released
            and LOW when pressed.  The pull-up has to charge the pin's stray
            capacitance first (<xref ref="fig-pullup-settling"/>), which takes a few
            microseconds; with the debounce capacitor of
            <xref ref="subsec-hw-debounce"/> fitted it takes milliseconds, which is why
            <c>button.c</c> waits 50 ms.  Test it with:
        </p>
        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Active-low: IDR bit = 0 means button is pressed
if ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0) {
    // button is pressed — take action here
}
]]></code></program>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-gpio-input">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-idr-value">
                <statement><p>With the internal pull-up enabled on PB4 and the button wired from
                D5 to GND, what value does <c>GPIOB-&gt;IDR &amp; BUTTON_PIN</c>
                return when the button is pressed?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>0 (zero)</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Correct.  Pressing the button pulls the pin to GND, so the IDR
                    bit is 0 (active-low).</p></feedback></choice>
                    <choice><statement><p>1</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The pin is HIGH (1) when the button is <em>released</em>, not pressed.</p></feedback></choice>
                    <choice><statement><p>It depends on the MODER setting</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>MODER selects input vs. output mode, but once in input mode the
                    IDR reflects the pin voltage regardless of PUPDR.</p></feedback></choice>
                    <choice><statement><p>Undefined, floating</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>The pull-up resistor prevents the pin from floating; it is
                    well-defined as HIGH when the switch is open.</p></feedback></choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         DAY 3X IN-CLASS  (Wednesday x-hour, 50 min, no pre-class reading)
         Her own Day03x deck opens on slide 2, "Review": what Tuesday covered,
         then "Today: back to the oscilloscope, more about switches and
         inputs."  The section introduction below is that slide (P-12 reuse).
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-switches-day3x">
        <title>Day 3x In-Class: Observing and Fixing Bounce</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                On Tuesday we made our first oscilloscope measurements with the AD2
                and Waveforms, and we wired a pushbutton to a pin of the STM32C031C6.
                Today we'll go back to the oscilloscope and look more closely at
                switches and inputs.  We'll put the scope on the button itself and
                see what actually happens on the pin when a finger presses it, and
                then we'll fix the switch bounce in hardware, by putting a capacitor
                across the switch.  After that we'll discuss ways to write the
                toggleLED program: polling gives you the button's level when what a
                toggle needs is the moment it changes, so we'll detect the edge
                instead.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- ── Part 1: Observing and Fixing Bounce (≈20 min) ───── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day3x-bounce">
            <title>Part 1: Observing and Fixing Bounce</title>
            <p>
                We'll start by watching the button.  Load <c>button.c</c> onto your
                Nucleo, or your own <c>toggleLED.c</c> if you finished the homework.
                We'll wire the AD2 with CH1 (orange) on D5 (PB4, the button) and CH2
                (blue) on D13 (PA5, the LED), as in
                <xref ref="fig-bounce-wiring"/>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-bounce-wiring">
                <caption>AD2 wiring for observing button bounce.  CH1 (orange)
                connects to D5/PB4 (button); CH2 (blue) connects to D13/PA5 (LED).
                Minus leads (white stripe) connect to GND.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-wiring.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <activity xml:id="act-day3x-observe-bounce">
                <title>Capturing Switch Bounce</title>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        In Waveforms, configure the trigger as shown in
                        <xref ref="fig-day3x-trigger-setup"/>: Source: Channel 1,
                        Type: Edge, Condition: Falling, Level: 1 V.
                        Set the time base to 20 µs/div.
                        (Depending on your Waveforms version, you may need
                        Normal or Auto trigger mode.)
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-day3x-trigger-setup">
                        <caption>Waveforms trigger settings for capturing switch bounce.
                        Source: Channel 1 (button signal), Condition: Falling edge,
                        Level: 1 V.  Time base 20 µs/div is a good starting point;
                        adjust if your bounce is shorter or longer.</caption>
                        <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-trigger.png" width="85%"/>
                    </figure>
                    <p>
                        Click <em>Single</em>, press the button once, and
                        examine the captured waveform.  What do you see
                        instead of a clean falling edge?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Use cursors to measure the total duration of the
                        bounce on your button.  Compare results with other
                        students.  Do all switches bounce the same amount?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Look at channel 2 (LED).  How many times does the
                        LED change state during a single physical button press?
                        Why?
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-bounce-scope">
                <caption>Switch bounce captured on CH1 (orange): a single button press
                produces many rapid HIGH/LOW transitions before the signal settles low.
                CH2 (blue) shows the LED toggling on each bounce, not just once.
                Bounce duration varies by switch, typically 50 µs to a few ms.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-bounce.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

            <!-- This paragraph exists so that the pointer to
                 subsec-hw-debounce lives in READING prose rather than inside
                 act-day3x-hw-debounce, which day3x.json projects.  PreTeXt
                 renders a section/subsection <xref> as class="internal", and
                 class.html's dexref() only neutralizes class="xref", so the
                 link survived onto the wall as a live click out of the deck
                 (L-21 / style-sweep C-5).  Keep it out of the activity. -->
            <p>
                Now we'll try the analog fix: a small capacitor across the switch.  The
                circuit and the arithmetic behind it are written out in
                <xref ref="subsec-hw-debounce"/>; here we'll just fit the part and look
                at what it does to the trace.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-day3x-hw-debounce">
                <title>Hardware Fix: Adding a Capacitor</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        In Lab 2's Technical Study you learn how to select an
                        appropriately sized capacitor for a given switch.  For
                        today the 0.1 µF or 0.15 µF capacitor works well.
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-cap-codes">
                        <caption>Reading metal film capacitor markings.
                        The three fields encode value, tolerance, and maximum voltage.
                        Value is two digits and a decimal point; the decimal point
                        can be an <c>n</c> (nano), a <c>p</c> (pico), or a dot (micro).
                        Example: <c>.1J63</c> = 0.1 µF, ±5%, 63 V.  The two photographs
                        are what the part looks like in your kit: a small squared-off
                        block with two legs, in blue or cream depending on the
                        manufacturer, with the marking printed on the flat face.</caption>
                        <sidebyside widths="66% 15% 15%" margins="0% 0%" valign="middle">
                            <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-cap-codes.png"/>
                            <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/metal-film-cap-blue.png"/>
                            <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/metal-film-cap-cream.png"/>
                        </sidebyside>
                    </figure>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-rc-debounce-circuit">
                        <caption>Place the debounce capacitor (0.1 µF or 0.15 µF,
                        depending on how your kit was stocked) from D5 (PB4) to GND on
                        the breadboard, in parallel with the button.  The red dashed line
                        shows where the capacitor connects.  Identify the correct
                        capacitor by reading its marking code (Task 1 below).</caption>
                        <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-rc-wiring.png" width="75%"/>
                    </figure>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Identify the debounce capacitor in your kit using its
                        marking code.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Place the capacitor across the button (one leg on
                        the GPIO pin side, the other to GND).  Capture the
                        button waveform again with the same scope settings.
                        How has it changed?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Look at channel 2 (LED) again.  Does the LED now
                        toggle exactly once per press?  Save a screenshot
                        showing both channels, with and without the capacitor,
                        for your lab report.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>



            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-wire" ref="fig-bounce-wiring">
                <ul>
                    <li>Load <c>button.c</c>, or your own <c>toggleLED.c</c> if you finished the homework.</li>
                    <li>CH1 (orange) on D5 / PB4 (button).</li>
                    <li>CH2 (blue) on D13 / PA5 (LED).</li>
                    <li>Minus (white-stripe) leads to GND.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>CH1 on the button (PB4), CH2 on the LED (PA5); minus leads to GND.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-trigger" ref="fig-day3x-trigger-setup" mediawide="yes">
                <ul>
                    <li>Source: Channel 1 · Type: Edge · Condition: Falling · Level: 1 V.</li>
                    <li>Time base: 20 µs/div to start.</li>
                    <li>Click Single, press the button once, and examine the capture.</li>
                    <li>(Depending on your Waveforms version, you may need Normal or Auto mode.)</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Trigger on Channel 1, falling edge, 1 V.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-bounce-look" ref="fig-bounce-scope">
                <caption>One press on CH1 (orange) makes many rapid HIGH/LOW transitions before settling. CH2 (blue) shows the LED toggling on each bounce, not just once. Bounce lasts ~50 µs to a few ms, and varies by switch.</caption>
                <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL — show after the activity if useful, or skip.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-hw-fix" ref="fig-day3x-rc" stack="yes">
                <p>The analog fix is to connect a small capacitor across the switch, from D5 (PB4) to GND.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>A capacitor cannot change its voltage instantly, so the pin's voltage can no longer follow the contacts as they chatter.</li>
                    <li>Fit the capacitor, capture the button again, and note the effect.  We'll work out why it works in a moment.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>The switch, the capacitor across it, and the internal pull-up that charges the capacitor while the switch is open.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-cap-codes" ref="fig-cap-codes">
                <caption>Metal-film capacitor markings: value, tolerance, and max voltage. The decimal point can be an <c>n</c> (nano), <c>p</c> (pico), or a dot (micro). Example: <c>.1J63</c> = 0.1 µF, ±5%, 63 V. In your kit it is the small squared-off block with two legs, blue or cream, with the marking on the flat face.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-cap-place" ref="fig-rc-debounce-circuit">
                <caption>Place the debounce capacitor (0.1 µF or 0.15 µF, kits have either) across the button, from D5 (PB4) to GND, in parallel with the switch.</caption>
            </slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-day3x-compare">
                <caption>Button waveform with vs. without the debounce capacitor.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-compare.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-with-without" ref="fig-day3x-compare">
                <caption>Adding the capacitor rounds off the fast bounce so the pin makes one clean transition, and the LED toggles once per press.</caption>
                <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL — show after the activity if useful, or skip.</note>
            </slide>

            <figure xml:id="fig-day3x-rc">
                <caption>The internal pull-up and the debounce capacitor form an RC low-pass filter.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-rc-circuit.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-rc-why" ref="fig-day3x-rc">
                <ul>
                    <li><term>Switch open.</term>  Recall from Phys 14 that a capacitor together with the internal pull-up (~40 kΩ) forms an RC low-pass filter.  Through the pull-up the capacitor charges slowly to 3.3 V, so once the threshold voltage is reached the pin reads HIGH.</li>
                    <li><term>Switch pressed.</term>  The closed contacts have almost no resistance, so the capacitor discharges through them at once and the pin goes LOW.</li>
                    <li><term>Contacts bouncing.</term>  Each time they break, the capacitor can only recharge through the 40 kΩ pull-up, which is slow.  If RC is large enough the voltage never climbs back above <m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m> before the contacts close again, so no spurious edges reach the pin.</li>
                    <li><term>Switch fully closed.</term>  The capacitor stays discharged and the pin sits at 0.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>OPTIONAL / REVEAL.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-hw-or-sw">
                <p>Bounce can be fixed in hardware or in software, and each way costs something different:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><term>Hardware</term>, an RC filter or a digital timer: it does not tie up processor cycles.  It costs another small component and its wiring, and it is hard to revise for a different switch, so it has to be designed with some tolerance.</li>
                    <li><term>Software</term>, a delay: it is flexible, because if the switch changes you edit the code and reflash.  It is simple, and it needs no extra components.  It costs processor cycles, spent waiting for the bouncing to stop.</li>
                    <li>Today we're using the hardware fix, the capacitor we just fitted.  A software delay would have done the same job.  In Lab 2's Technical Study you'll investigate this more.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 2: The Toggle Problem (≈15 min) ────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day3x-toggle-problem">
            <title>Part 2: The Toggle Problem</title>
            <p>
                The capacitor fixed the bounce, but there is a second problem that a
                capacitor cannot fix.  Reading a pin on every
                iteration of the main loop, checking its state and acting on it, is
                called <term>polling</term>.  It is the simplest way to respond to an
                input, and it is what we have been doing since <c>button.c</c>.  A more
                general way to write it is to separate reading the pin from acting on
                what was read, which is what <c>buttonPushed</c> does below.  Polling
                stops being enough as soon as the action we want is a toggle, because a
                pin gives you a level and a toggle needs an edge; this next activity is
                where you find out what that costs.  Even with a debounced switch,
                consider this loop:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (1) {
    buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
    if (buttonPushed) {
        GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN;   // toggle LED
    }
}
]]></code></program>
            <activity xml:id="act-day3x-toggle-problem">
                <title>The Toggle Problem</title>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Predict what this code will do if you press and hold
                        the button for half a second.  How many times will the
                        LED toggle?  Why?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        The loop runs at approximately 12 MHz.  Roughly how
                        many times does it execute while a human holds the
                        button down for 200 ms?  How many toggles does that
                        produce?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        The fix is to detect the <em>transition</em> from
                        unpressed to pressed, not just the level.  The code
                        below uses a <c>buttonAlreadyPressed</c> flag to do
                        this.  Trace through it: when does the LED toggle?
                        When does it not toggle even though the button is down?
                    </p>
                    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
unsigned buttonAlreadyPressed = 0;
while (1) {
    buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
    if (!buttonAlreadyPressed && buttonPushed) {
        GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN;      // toggle on leading edge only
        buttonAlreadyPressed = 1;
    } else if (!buttonPushed) {
        buttonAlreadyPressed = 0;   // reset when released
    }
}
]]></code></program>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        On Thursday we'll write this same logic a cleaner way, using
                        the <em>state machine</em> design pattern.  Read
                        <xref ref="sec-state-machine"/> before then, and we'll
                        discuss in class how its <c>state</c> variable corresponds to
                        the <c>buttonAlreadyPressed</c> flag above.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>



            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-naive-polling">
                <ul>
                    <li>Reading the pin on every pass of the main loop and acting on its value is called <term>polling</term>. It is the simplest way to respond to an input.</li>
                    <li>A more general way to write it is to separate reading the pin from acting on what was read, which is what <c>buttonPushed</c> does below.</li>
                    <li>Even with a debounced switch, this loop does not achieve the desired toggling action.  Why?</li>
                </ul>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (1) {
    buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
    if (buttonPushed) {
        GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN;   // toggle LED
    }
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-toggle-activity">
                <ul>
                    <li>Predict: if you press and hold the button for half a second, how many times will the LED toggle? Why?</li>
                    <li>The loop runs at ~12 MHz. Roughly how many times does it execute while a human holds the button for 200 ms? How many toggles does that produce?</li>
                    <li>The fix is to act on the moment the button goes down, rather than on the fact that it is down. The skeleton on the next slide does this; fill in the missing lines.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-edge-skeleton">
                <p>Often you need to sense both states of the switch, not just its current level.  The fix is to detect the leading edge, the moment the pin goes LOW, then remember once you have acted on it, so that the next pass of the loop does not act again.  Fill in the blanks:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
unsigned buttonAlreadyPressed = 0;
while (1) {
    buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
    if (!buttonAlreadyPressed && buttonPushed) {
        // TODO: toggle the LED
        // TODO: remember that the button is now pressed
    } else if (!buttonPushed) {
        // TODO: reset so the next press can be detected
    }
}
]]></code></program>
                <note>Students complete the three TODO lines. Full solution is the hidden instructor slide at the end.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day3x-toggle-solution" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN  (1U<<0)
#define GPIOBEN  (1U<<1)
#define LED_PIN     (1U<<5)   // PA5 / D13
#define BUTTON_PIN  (1U<<4)   // PB4 / D5
int main(void) {
    unsigned buttonAlreadyPressed = 0, buttonPushed;
    RCC->IOPENR |= (GPIOAEN | GPIOBEN);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10);  GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11);  // PA5 output
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U<<8) & ~(1U<<9);                   // PB4 input
    GPIOB->PUPDR |= (1U<<8);   GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U<<9);   // PB4 pull-up
    while (1) {
        buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
        if (!buttonAlreadyPressed) {
            if (buttonPushed) { GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN; buttonAlreadyPressed = 1; }
        } else if (!buttonPushed) {
            buttonAlreadyPressed = 0;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-bounce">
        <title>Contact Bounce</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                When a mechanical switch closes, the metal contacts physically collide and
                spring apart several times before settling into steady contact.  This produces
                a burst of rapid HIGH/LOW transitions that can last anywhere from a few
                microseconds to 20 ms, depending on the switch.  To the STM32C031C6 running at
                12 MHz, each bounce looks like a separate button press.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-hw-debounce">
            <title>Hardware Debouncing: RC Filter</title>
            <p>
                The classic hardware fix is to add a small capacitor (0.1 µF or 0.15 µF) across
                the switch terminals.  When the switch closes, the capacitor discharges through
                the low-resistance closed contacts instead of bouncing.  When the switch opens,
                the internal pull-up recharges the capacitor through its 40 kΩ resistance.
                The resulting RC time constant smooths out the bounce spikes.
                Breadboard placement is shown in <xref ref="fig-rc-debounce-circuit"/>.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-rc-schematic">
                <caption>RC debounce circuit: the internal pull-up resistance
                <m>R_{\mathrm{pu}}</m>, about 40 kΩ, and the external capacitor form an RC
                low-pass filter.  When the switch bounces open, the capacitor recharges
                slowly through <m>R_{\mathrm{pu}}</m>; if C is large enough, the voltage never
                rises above <m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m> during the bounce window.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-rc-circuit.png" width="75%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-rc-results">
                <caption>Effect of capacitor size on bounce suppression.  Top: C = 0.01 µF,
                bounce recovers quickly and can reach V<sub>IH</sub>, causing spurious edges.
                Bottom: C = 0.1 µF, bounce recovers so slowly that the voltage stays
                below V<sub>IH</sub>, eliminating spurious transitions.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-rc-results.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-bounce-comparison">
                <caption>Side-by-side oscilloscope view: without capacitor (left) vs. with
                0.1 µF (right).  Orange (CH1) = button signal; blue (CH2) = LED.
                Without debouncing, the LED toggles multiple times per press.
                With the capacitor, each press produces exactly one toggle.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/ann-d3x-compare.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-sw-debounce">
            <title>Software Debouncing</title>
            <p>
                The simplest software debounce is to wait, after detecting a press, for
                longer than the switch's maximum bounce duration, and then read the pin
                again to confirm that it is still pressed.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
if (!(GPIOC->IDR & BTN_PIN)) {     // button pressed (active-low)
    delay_ms(20);                   // wait for bounce to settle (>10 ms typical)
    if (!(GPIOC->IDR & BTN_PIN)) { // confirm still pressed
        GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN;      // take action
        while (!(GPIOC->IDR & BTN_PIN)); // wait for release
        delay_ms(20);               // debounce the release too
    }
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                This works but <em>blocks</em> the CPU for 20 ms twice per button press.
                The same waiting can be done without blocking, by a state machine that
                starts a timer on the first edge and ignores the pin until it expires, or
                one that requires the pin to hold the same value for several consecutive
                samples.  That is a different machine from the two-state toggle in
                <xref ref="sec-state-machine"/>, which turns a level into an edge and
                does not debounce.
            </p>

            <insight>
                <title>Hardware or software?</title>
                <p>
                    Hardware debouncing, with an RC filter or a digital timer, does not
                    tie up processor cycles.  What it costs is another small component
                    on the board and the wiring for it, and it is hard to revise for a
                    different switch, so it has to be designed with some tolerance.
                    Software debouncing is flexible: if the switch is changed, you edit
                    the code and reflash.  It is also simple, and it needs no extra
                    components.  What it costs is processor cycles, spent waiting for
                    the bouncing to stop.  In practice a small capacitor together with
                    a short software delay covers both sides.  In Lab 2's Technical
                    Study you learn how to select an appropriately sized capacitor for a
                    given switch.
                </p>
            </insight>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-state-machine">
        <title>State Machine Design Pattern</title>
        <p>
            This is the reading for Day 4.  The goal is for you to arrive able to draw
            a state diagram, because on Thursday we'll turn your own button code into a
            state machine and then design new ones from scratch.
        </p>
        <p>
            A <term>finite state machine</term> (FSM) is a way of thinking about code
            that is especially useful for event-driven programs such as those running on
            embedded systems.  It describes a program as a finite number of possible
            states, with transitions between them that capture the behavior and the
            logic.  Think for example of a simple traffic light.  It has three states:
            red, green and yellow.  (Things get more complex once we add states such as
            red blinking or yellow blinking, so we won't do that here.)  Once the light
            is in state red it stays there until a certain amount of time has passed, at
            which point it transitions to the green state.  (Let's keep this simple too
            and not think about traffic-aware lights.)  The timer going off to let the
            light know it is time to change is what we would call an <term>event</term>,
            and the switch from red to green a <term>transition</term>.  Once the light
            is green it stays in that new <term>state</term> until another event occurs,
            namely another timer that indicates it is time to switch to yellow.  The
            timer going off is the event again, and the transition is the switch to
            yellow.  Next, yellow transitions back to red after another amount of time
            has passed.
        </p>
        <p>
            We'll use this pattern in our code.  We keep track of what state our system
            is currently in via a state variable, and in our infinite loop we check only
            whether an event has happened that merits a state transition.  So we never
            wait for a transition signal in a blocking <c>while</c> loop.
        </p>
        <p>
            So a <term>state</term> is what the
            program remembers about where it currently is: not the value of a pin right
            now, but which of a small number of situations it is currently in.  An
            <term>event</term> is something that happens and that the program notices,
            such as a pin going LOW or an interval elapsing.  A
            <term>transition</term> is the move from one state to another that an event
            causes, and the <term>action</term> is whatever the program does on the way.
            A state machine is nothing more than a list of the states, and for each one,
            which events move it to what new state and what happens along the way.
        </p>
        <p>
            Recall <c>blinkySEQ.c</c> from Lab 1: the program lights three
            LEDs in turn, and we can think of that as a machine with three states, one
            for each LED (<xref ref="fig-fsm-blinkyseq"/>).  The only event is the
            half-second interval elapsing.  Each transition's action is to turn the LED
            that is currently on off, light the next one, and restart the timer; after
            the third it wraps back to the first.  Written as a diagram, circles are the
            states, arrows are the transitions, and each arrow carries the event that
            triggers it and then, after a slash, the action taken.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-fsm-blinkyseq">
            <caption>The running light of <c>blinkySEQ.c</c> drawn as a state diagram.
            Three states, one per LED; one event, the half-second interval elapsing; and
            three things done on each transition, which are to turn the LED that is on
            off, light the next one, and restart the timer.  The machine wraps from the
            third state back to the first, which is what makes the light run
            continuously.</caption>
            <image source="images/Day04-Debounce/fsm-blinkyseq.svg" width="92%"/>
        </figure>
        <p>
            Notice what the diagram makes obvious and what is a bit harder to see from code alone: there are
            exactly three situations the program can be in, and exactly one thing that
            can move it between them.  That is the value of drawing a state machine before writing
            any code.  A blinking sequence is simple enough that a
            <c>for</c> loop handles it, but as soon as two different events can arrive,
            such as an interval elapsing and a button going down, the loop stops being
            enough and the diagram is what helps you keep the behavior straight.
        </p>
        <p>
            For a button toggle, we need two states: <c>UNPRESSED</c> and <c>PRESSED</c>.
            We toggle the LED exactly once per press, when transitioning from UNPRESSED to
            PRESSED.  Once in the PRESSED state we ignore further LOW readings until the
            pin goes HIGH again, which is what stops a finger held down for half a second
            from toggling the LED thousands of times.  It does not cure bounce: while the
            contacts are chattering the pin really does go back above
            <m>V_{\mathrm{IH}}</m> and LOW again, and each of those is a genuine
            UNPRESSED-to-PRESSED transition that toggles the LED once more.  That is what
            the capacitor, or a delay in software, is for.
        </p>
        <p>
            Before coming to class, draw the state diagram for the button-toggle FSM
            described above on paper, the same way the running light is drawn in
            <xref ref="fig-fsm-blinkyseq"/>.  Label each state, each transition arrow
            with its triggering event, and the action taken on it.
        </p>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-fsm">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-fsm-states">
                <statement><p>In a button-toggle FSM with states UNPRESSED and PRESSED,
                the program is in state PRESSED and the button is still held down (pin LOW).
                What should happen on the next pass through the main loop?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>Nothing: stay in PRESSED and do not toggle the LED.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Correct.  The PRESSED state only acts when the button is released,
                    so holding the button causes no additional toggles.</p></feedback></choice>
                    <choice><statement><p>The LED toggles again.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>That would happen with a flag-based approach that toggles on
                    every LOW reading.  The state machine prevents repeated toggles by staying
                    in PRESSED until the button is released.</p></feedback></choice>
                    <choice><statement><p>The state transitions back to UNPRESSED.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>PRESSED → UNPRESSED only when the pin goes HIGH (button released).</p></feedback></choice>
                    <choice><statement><p>The program gets stuck in an infinite loop.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>There is no blocking loop inside the state machine; it returns
                    to the top of <c>while(1)</c> each cycle.</p></feedback></choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================
         DAY 4 IN-CLASS  (Thursday, 110 min)
         Pre-class reading: sec-state-machine (state machines)
         The part budgets below sum to 90 minutes in a 110-minute Thursday.
         Do not "correct" the class length to match the sum: CLAUDE.md fixes
         Day N with N even at 110 minutes.
         ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-switches-day4">
        <title>Day 4 In-Class: State Machines</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll turn the toggle logic into a state machine.  The homework
                you brought in already tracks whether the button was down last time
                around the loop; that variable is a <term>state</term>, and the pattern
                built on it is the <term>finite state machine</term>, which both Lab 2
                design challenges need.  We'll start by reading each other's toggle
                code, then write the same logic as a state diagram and as C,
                then design two machines of our own on paper and implement one of
                them.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- ── Part 1: Code Review (≈10 min) ──────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day4-code-review">
            <title>Part 1: Code Review (toggleLED.c)</title>
            <p>
                We'll start with the homework you brought in.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day4-code-review">
                <title>toggleLED Code Review</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Set up your Nucleo with your <c>toggleLED.c</c> (debounce capacitor across the switch) and share it with your group.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Read each partner's code.  Can you follow its logic
                        and explain it line by line?  There are at least two
                        valid structures using a <c>buttonAlreadyPressed</c>
                        flag: one checks the flag in the outer <c>if</c>,
                        the other checks <c>buttonPushed</c> first.  Are
                        both correct?  Is one clearer?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Identify the parts of your code that correspond to FSM concepts:
                        where is the <em>state</em> stored?  What are the <em>events</em>?
                        What <em>action</em> happens on which transition?
                        We will formalize this next.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>


            <slide xml:id="sl-day4-sol1" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN (1U<<0)
#define GPIOBEN (1U<<1)
#define LED_PIN    (1U<<5)   // PA5 / D13
#define BUTTON_PIN (1U<<4)   // PB4 / D5
int main(void) {
    unsigned buttonAlreadyPressed = 0, buttonPushed;
    RCC->IOPENR |= (GPIOAEN | GPIOBEN);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10);  GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11);   // PA5 output
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U<<8) & ~(1U<<9);                    // PB4 input
    GPIOB->PUPDR |= (1U<<8);   GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U<<9);    // PB4 pull-up
    while (1) {
        buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
        if (!buttonAlreadyPressed) {
            if (buttonPushed) { GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN; buttonAlreadyPressed = 1; }
        } else {
            if (!buttonPushed) buttonAlreadyPressed = 0;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day4-sol2" instructor="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN (1U<<0)
#define GPIOBEN (1U<<1)
#define LED_PIN    (1U<<5)   // PA5 / D13
#define BUTTON_PIN (1U<<4)   // PB4 / D5
int main(void) {
    unsigned buttonAlreadyPressed = 0, buttonPushed;
    RCC->IOPENR |= (GPIOAEN | GPIOBEN);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10);  GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11);   // PA5 output
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U<<8) & ~(1U<<9);                    // PB4 input
    GPIOB->PUPDR |= (1U<<8);   GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U<<9);    // PB4 pull-up
    while (1) {
        buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
        if (buttonPushed) {
            if (buttonAlreadyPressed == 0) {
                GPIOA->ODR ^= LED_PIN; buttonAlreadyPressed = 1;
            }
        } else {
            buttonAlreadyPressed = 0;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 2: FSM Mini-Lecture (≈15 min) ────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day4-fsm-lecture">
            <title>Part 2: The State Machine Pattern</title>
            <p>
                Next we'll put a name to what your code is already doing.  Your
                <c>toggleLED.c</c> implements a finite state machine: the
                <c>buttonAlreadyPressed</c> variable <em>is</em> the state variable, and
                the two branches of the <c>if</c> are its two states.  The button can be
                pressed or unpressed, but it is only when we observe a
                <term>transition</term> that the program needs to do anything: update
                the state, and toggle the LED on the transition from unpressed to
                pressed.  We'll write that
                same logic out twice, once as a formal state diagram and once as
                C using <c>typedef enum</c> and <c>switch</c>, so that the
                diagram and the code can be read against each other line by line.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-state-diagram">
                <caption>State diagram for the button-toggle finite state machine (FSM).  The two states
                (Unpressed/Pressed) track whether the button is currently down.
                The LED toggles exactly once (on the Unpressed→Pressed transition),
                and the code snippet on each arrow is the C expression that triggers it.
                The legend (right) maps the color-coded terms to the FSM vocabulary.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day04-Debounce/ann-d4-state-diagram.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"

typedef enum { UNPRESSED, PRESSED } state_t;

int main(void) {
    // --- GPIO setup (GPIOA: LED on PA5; GPIOB: button on PB4) ---
    RCC->IOPENR |= (1U << 0) | (1U << 1);  // enable GPIOA and GPIOB clocks
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 10);            // PA5 input (clear)
    GPIOA->MODER |=  (1U << 10);            // PA5 output
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(3U << 8);             // PB4 input (pins reset to analog)
    GPIOB->PUPDR |=  (1U << 8);             // PB4 pull-up bit [9:8] = 01
    GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U << 9);

    state_t state = UNPRESSED;

    while (1) {
        int btn = !(GPIOB->IDR & (1U << 4)); // 1 if pressed (active-low)

        switch (state) {
            case UNPRESSED:
                if (btn) {
                    GPIOA->ODR ^= (1U << 5); // toggle LED on press
                    state = PRESSED;
                }
                break;
            case PRESSED:
                if (!btn) {
                    state = UNPRESSED;        // wait for release
                }
                break;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                Because there is no blocking <c>while</c> loop, this FSM can be embedded
                in a larger main loop that also blinks LEDs, reads a UART, or does other
                work: the state variable preserves context across iterations.
            </p>


            <slide xml:id="sl-day4-fsm-diagram" ref="fig-state-diagram">
                <ul>
                    <li>The <c>buttonAlreadyPressed</c> variable is the state.</li>
                    <li>Two states: Unpressed / Pressed.</li>
                    <li>The LED toggles exactly once, on the Unpressed → Pressed transition.</li>
                    <li>The snippet on each arrow is the C expression that triggers it.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Button-toggle FSM: two states, with the triggering C expression on each transition.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day4-enum-switch">
                <p>The same logic in C: <c>typedef enum</c> + <c>switch</c>:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
typedef enum { UNPRESSED, PRESSED } state_t;
state_t state = UNPRESSED;
while (1) {
    int btn = !(GPIOB->IDR & (1U << 4));   // 1 if pressed (active-low)
    switch (state) {
    case UNPRESSED:
        if (btn) {
            GPIOA->ODR ^= (1U << 5);   // toggle LED on press
            state = PRESSED;
        }
        break;
    case PRESSED:
        if (!btn) state = UNPRESSED;    // wait for release
        break;
    }
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day4-why">
                <ul>
                    <li>A finite state machine is a set of <term>states</term>, the <term>events</term> that cause transitions between them, and the <term>actions</term> taken on each transition. Its advantage over a single flag variable is that it keeps working as you add more states or more events.</li>
                    <li>No blocking <c>while</c> loop inside: the FSM does a little work each pass and returns.</li>
                    <li>So it embeds inside a larger main loop that can blink LEDs, read a UART, or do other work.</li>
                    <li>The state variable preserves context across iterations.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 3: State Machine Design (≈20 min) ─────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day4-fsm-design">
            <title>Part 3: Designing State Machines</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll design two machines that are not the toggle.  A single flag
                gives you two states and no room for a third, while adding a state or
                an event to a state diagram costs a circle or an arrow.  We'll draw
                both machines before we write any code, because a machine with a
                missing arrow is much easier to see on paper than in a <c>switch</c>.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day4-fsm-draw">
                <title>Drawing a State Diagram</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Before writing any code, draw the state diagram on
                        paper or your table's whiteboard.  Label each state
                        (box), each transition arrow with its triggering event,
                        and each action taken on the transition.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Draw the state diagram for a 3-state LED cycling FSM:
                        each button press advances to the next LED pattern
                        (pattern A → B → C → A → …).  You choose the patterns.
                        How many states do you need?  What are the events?
                        What action happens on each transition?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Draw the state diagram for the Lab 2 pause/resume
                        counter: the counter blinks normally until a button
                        press pauses it; a second press resumes.
                        How is this different from the 3-state cycling FSM?
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day4-fsm-diagrams">
                <p>
                    <term>The 3-state cycling FSM.</term>  Three states, one per
                    pattern, and one event.
                </p>
                <p><ul>
                    <li><em>States:</em> PATTERN_A, PATTERN_B, PATTERN_C.</li>
                    <li><em>Event:</em> a button <em>press</em>, the moment the
                        pin goes LOW, not the fact that it is LOW.</li>
                    <li><em>Actions:</em> on A→B write pattern B to
                        <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c>; on B→C write pattern C; on C→A write
                        pattern A.  The action is on the transition, and each of
                        the three arrows carries a different one.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p>
                    <term>Why three states are not enough.</term>  Three states
                    only work if the event really is <q>a press</q>.  A pin does
                    not give you a press; it gives you a level, and turning a
                    level into an edge is itself the two-state machine from
                    earlier today.  So a working cycling FSM is either six states
                    (each pattern with a <q>button still down</q> twin) or three
                    pattern states running alongside the Unpressed/Pressed machine
                    that supplies the edge.  A student who draws three states and
                    no release arrow has drawn a machine that advances the pattern
                    thousands of times per press.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>The pause/resume counter.</term>  Two states, RUNNING
                    and PAUSED, with the same press event flipping between them,
                    and the same edge problem, so four states in practice.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>How the two differ.</term>  In the cycling FSM the work
                    happens <em>on the transitions</em>: each arrow writes a
                    pattern and then nothing happens until the next press.  In the pause/resume
                    counter the work happens <em>in a state</em>: RUNNING keeps
                    counting for as long as it is the current state, and the
                    transitions do nothing but start and stop it.  Same diagram
                    shape, opposite placement of the action.
                </p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 4: Implementing State Machines (≈30 min) ───── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day4-fsm-code">
            <title>Part 4: Implementing a State Machine in C</title>
            <p>
                Now we'll turn one of the diagrams you just drew into working code,
                using the <c>typedef enum</c> and <c>switch</c> pattern from earlier
                today.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day4-fsm-implement">
                <title>Implementing Your FSM</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Start from your <c>toggleLED.c</c> or <c>blinkyCNT.c</c>
                        project.  Replace any blocking logic or bare flag
                        variables with a proper FSM.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Implement the pause/resume counter FSM.  The counter
                        should blink the LEDs in binary (as in <c>blinkyCNT.c</c>)
                        and a button press should pause or resume counting.
                        Test it: does a single physical press always produce
                        exactly one state change?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        If you finish early, implement the 3-state LED cycling
                        FSM from your diagram.  Or: add a second button that
                        resets the counter to zero from either state.
                        How does the state diagram change?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        Verify on the oscilloscope that the LED transitions
                        are clean (no multiple toggles per press) both with
                        and without the debounce capacitor.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <instructor xml:id="inst-day4-fsm-code">
                <p>
                    Here is the pause/resume counter written as a state machine.
                    It needs four states, because the two that matter (counting or
                    not) each need a twin that means <q>and the button is still
                    down</q>, and that twin is what makes one physical press
                    produce exactly one change.  The counter runs in the two RUN
                    states and stops in the two PAUSE ones.
                </p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#define GPIOAEN    (1U<<0)
#define GPIOBEN    (1U<<1)
#define LED1_BIT   5
#define LED_PIN1   (1U<<LED1_BIT)
#define LED_PIN2   (1U<<(LED1_BIT+1))
#define LED_PIN3   (1U<<(LED1_BIT+2))
#define LED_ALL    (LED_PIN1 | LED_PIN2 | LED_PIN3)
#define BUTTON_PIN (1U<<4)          // PB4 / D5

typedef enum { RUN, RUN_HELD, PAUSE, PAUSE_HELD } state_t;

int main(void) {
    state_t  state = RUN;
    unsigned count = 0, buttonPushed;

    RCC->IOPENR |= (GPIOAEN | GPIOBEN);
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U<<10)|(1U<<12)|(1U<<14);        // PA5,6,7 outputs
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U<<11) & ~(1U<<13) & ~(1U<<15);
    GPIOB->MODER &= ~(1U<<8) & ~(1U<<9);               // PB4 input
    GPIOB->PUPDR |= (1U<<8);                           // PB4 pull-up
    GPIOB->PUPDR &= ~(1U<<9);

    while (1) {
        // Half a second, read fifty times instead of once: delay_ms(500) would
        // spend the whole interval unable to see the button.
        for (unsigned slice = 0; slice < 50; slice++) {
            buttonPushed = ((GPIOB->IDR & BUTTON_PIN) == 0);
            switch (state) {
                case RUN:
                    if (buttonPushed)  state = PAUSE_HELD;  // stops immediately
                    break;
                case PAUSE_HELD:
                    if (!buttonPushed) state = PAUSE;       // finger lifted
                    break;
                case PAUSE:
                    if (buttonPushed)  state = RUN_HELD;
                    break;
                case RUN_HELD:
                    if (!buttonPushed) state = RUN;
                    break;
            }
            delay_ms(10);
        }
        if (state == RUN || state == RUN_HELD) {
            count = (count + 1) % 8;
            GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_ALL;
            GPIOA->ODR |= count << LED1_BIT;
        }
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
                <p>
                    <term>What the third task finds.</term>  With the debounce
                    capacitor fitted, one press gives one state change and the
                    scope shows a single clean transition.  Without it, a press
                    can bounce across two reads 10 ms apart and the counter
                    pauses and resumes again inside one press, visible as a
                    missed pause rather than as a double count, which is why it is
                    worth watching the LEDs and the trace together.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <term>Why the delay is sliced.</term>  A student who keeps
                    <c>delay_ms(500)</c> and puts the switch after it has built
                    something that reads the button twice a second, so a normal
                    press lands in the gap and does nothing at all.  It is worth
                    letting that happen once, because it is the same defect we
                    open Day 9 with.  Slicing the wait is the answer available
                    today; on Day 9 we'll replace the whole arrangement with an
                    interrupt.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The stretch (a second button that resets the counter from
                    either state) does not add states.  It adds one event and two
                    self-transitions, one on RUN and one on PAUSE, each with the
                    action <c>count = 0</c>, and the machine returns to the state
                    it was already in.
                </p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 5: Lab 2 Introduction (≈15 min) ────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day4-lab2-intro">
            <title>Part 5: Lab 2 Introduction</title>
            <p>
                In the rest of the hour we'll start on Lab 2, which has two design
                challenges.  Read the full spec in <xref ref="sec-switch-lab"/> and
                start work on whichever challenge you have not already built today.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day4-lab2-start">
                <title>Lab 2 Design Challenges</title>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        <em>Challenge 1: Pause/resume counter:</em>
                        If the FSM you implemented earlier today already does this,
                        review the lab spec and make sure your solution
                        meets all requirements (correct state behavior,
                        debouncing, clean oscilloscope traces).
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task>
                    <p>
                        <em>Challenge 2: Reaction time game:</em>
                        Draw the state diagram before writing code.
                        How many states does the game have?  What are the
                        events (button presses, timer expiry)?  What actions
                        does each transition trigger?
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>
        </subsection>

    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-switch-lab">
        <title>Lab 2: Switches and State Machines</title>
        <p>
            Lab 2 has two parts: a Technical Study on RC debouncer design (prelab math),
            and two Design Challenges implemented on the Nucleo.  Read the full lab handout
            for the prelab deliverables.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lab2-setup">
            <caption>Lab 2 breadboard setup: three LEDs (two red WIN LEDs and one green
            starter LED), two pushbutton switches, and debounce capacitors.  Each LED has
            its own 220 Ω current-limiting resistor.</caption>
            <image source="images/Day03x-Debounce/slide20_cdfa0eb4.png" width="70%"/>
        </figure>

        <exercises>
            <exercise xml:id="exer-pause-resume">
                <title>Design Challenge 1: Pushbutton Counter</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Starting from <c>blinkyCNT.c</c>, implement a button-controlled
                        pause/resume: pressing the button once pauses the LED counter;
                        pressing again resumes it.  Use your debounce capacitor
                        (0.1 µF or 0.15 µF) across the switch.  Use the FSM pattern
                        to avoid blocking.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="exer-reaction-game">
                <title>Design Challenge 2: Reaction Time Game</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Each player has a debounced button and a red WIN LED.
                        A third green LED is the start signal.
                        The game runs as follows:
                    </p>
                    <p><ol>
                        <li><p>Initially, the green starter LED is on (or blinking rapidly).</p></li>
                        <li><p>Either player presses their button to start the game.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The starter LED flashes three times, one second apart,
                        then glows steadily: this is the go signal.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Each player presses their button as soon as they see the
                        steady light.  The first player to press wins; their WIN LED lights
                        (or blinks rapidly) for one second.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The system returns to step 1.</p></li>
                    </ol></p>
                    <p>
                        Design with a state machine.  Start with a paper state diagram
                        before writing any code; include it in your submission along with
                        pseudocode and a debugging diary.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <em>Optional extensions (no extra points):</em>
                        (a) A false start, pressing before the steady light, awards
                        the win to the other player.
                        (b) Make the delay between the last flash and the steady ON
                        slightly random (run a fast counter in the background and
                        sample it when a player starts the game) to prevent timing the flashes.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>
    </section>

</chapter>
